he office exhausted. I've been forced to smother all day, constricted by [my] tie.\par 3. The boss is-in-the-habit-of tyrannizing his employees \u8230\'c9 and [my] new shoes [hurt my feet] (}{\i\f211 to-me feet burn}{\f208 ).\par 4. At home, in sandals and without a tie, I was hoping to rest (}{\i\f211 fut. inf.}{\f208 ). You are upsetting all my plans.\par 5.\emdash  Honey (}{\i\f211 mine}{\f208 ), why do you speak so? Don't you know [that] my mother (}{\i\f211 today in-the-evening}{\f208 ) [will be receiving her] daughters and sons-in-law [this evening] (}{\i\f211 to herself going-to-receive}{\f208 )? (}{\i\f211 future part.}{\f208 ).\par 6.\emdash  I had forgotten (}{\i\f211 of}{\f208 ) this bother.\par 7.\emdash  Remember! On Sunday she invited us [around] (}{\i\f211 for}{\f208 ) the day after tomorrow, [and] you yourself [promised] (}{\i\f211 accepted}{\f208 ).\par 8.\emdash  You spoke [truly] (}{\i\f211 true-things}{\f208 ). What's more, yesterday I ran into her in the King's Road, [and she] (}{\i\f211 who}{\f208 ) said to me:\par 9. \ldblquote Dearest son-in-law, (}{\i\f211 for}{\f208 ) what a great joy it will be to us to see you[pl.] again tomorrow\rdblquote ; and I did not demur.\par 10. Besides, [I should have known] (}{\i\f211 me to-flee not it-ought}{\f208 ) [that my] mother-in-law is [in the habit of receiving guests] on Tuesday[s] (}{\i\f211 to-greetings herself to-give to-be-accustomed}{\f208 ).\par 11.\emdash  [You have a bad memory] (}{\i\f211 By dull memory you-are}{\f208 ) \u8230\'c9 if [it has to do with] (}{\i\f211 concerning}{\f208 ) bothering things (}{\i\f211 it-is-dealt [with]}{\f208 ).\par \par 12. The days of the week are called: Monday (}{\i\f211 Moon's day}{\f208 ), Tuesday (}{\i\f211 Mars' day, the Anglo-Saxon Tyr}{\f208 ), Wednesday (}{\i\f211 Mercury's day, the Germanic Woden or Odin}{\f208 ), Thursday (}{\i\f211 Jupiter's day, i.e. Thor}{\f208 ), Friday (}{\i\f211 Venus' day, the goddess Frige or Freyja}{\f208 ), Saturday (}{\i\f211 Saturn's day}{\f208 ), [and] day of the Lord or Sunday (}{\i\f211 Sun\rquote s day}{\f208 ).\par 13. The first six days are dedicated to pagan gods, viz.: to the Moon, to Mars, to Mercury, to Juppiter, to Venus, [and] to Saturn.\par 14. The seventh day on the other hand was dedicated to the Lord by the Christians because \ldblquote on the seventh day God ended his work which he had made, and he rested\rdblquote  (Book of Genesis II,2).\par \par NOTES\par }{\b\f210 1. Gu}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 do}{\f208 , gen. }{\b\f210 Guid}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 nis}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 Guy}{\f208  (a French name, Italian }{\i\f211 Guido}{\f208 ).\par }{\b\f210 2. Foc}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 le}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 is}{\f208 , n., }{\i\f211 item worn around the throat}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 f}{\f208 au}{\b\f210 ces}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 ium}{\f208 , f. pl.).\par }{\b\f210 3. \u198\'aestu}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 re}{\f208  (1}{\f208\super st}{\f208  conj. present ind.: ae}{\b\f210 stuo}{\f208 , ae}{\b\f210 stuas}{\f208 , etc.) }{\i\f211 to be hot}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to smother}{\f208 , a verb which is good to know and which has a commonly used derivative which we will find tomorrow.\par }{\b\f210 4. C}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 gi}{\f208 , passive of }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 go}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 is}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 ere}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 co}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 gi}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 co}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 ctum}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to force to\u8230\'c9}{\f208 , which we have seen in L. 30, P. 6. The perfect participle clearly shows the origin of the verb (}{\b\f210 cum }{\f208 +}{\b\f210  }{\f208 a}{\b\f210 gere}{\f208 ).\par }{\b\f210 5.}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 Ad}{\f208 )}{\b\f210 min}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 ster}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 tri}{\f208 , m. (f. (}{\b\f210 ad}{\f208 )}{\b\f210 min}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 stra}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 ae}{\f208 ), }{\i\f211 servant}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 employee}{\f208 : ministers are, or should be, the }{\i\f211 servants}{\f208  of the State, of rites, etc.\par }{\b\f210 6. }